"**Simulation Engineer Orumm**  
  
*Log 01.04*  
When Director Kuda took over the Inquest, I knew it was my moment to shine. The second we arrived in these dismal depths, I implored that I be placed on the Simulation Krewe to devise a counter to Vloxx's enormous power. Unlike her predecessor, Director Kuda did not dismiss my ideas. Quite the opposite—she listened as I explained my line of thinking. While she felt the approach was unorthodox, she appreciated my ability to dream beyond traditional solutions... However, my first attempt performed...poorly. Feedback was negative across the board. I know the idea is sound, but I must find a way to increase user tolerance to the data-input stage.  
  
*Log 01.07*  
The wretches I must put up with. Technician Lammi came by with her own idea about how to adapt the simulation to ""achieve a more optional user experience."" Bookah. Her ridiculous suggestion? She said that the simulation display reminded her of a snake, eating its way through a field of infinite sources of food. The fool...  
  
*Log 02.03*  
After much consternation, I must begrudgingly retract my previous criticism of Technician Lammi's suggestion. While working on the latest rendering of the power consumption progression markers, her comments found their way into my consciousness, and I felt the only way to purge such interference would be to implement her idea—if for no other reason than to show her the smallness of her thinking. But, to my surprise, the other technicians and engineers came over to watch. They quickly became obsessed with seeing how many of my ""nutrition nodes"" they could consume before succumbing to the simulation. The next few days will prove...interesting.  
  
*Log 02.09*  
Serpent Simulator, as it has since been renamed, has become a phenomenal success! There is no shortage of subjects who are eager to participate, and while their interest is an unexpected byproduct of the ""fun"" of the simulation, every user is another set of data points to add to my study of perpetual power consumption. I know such research will lead us to finding a way to stop Vloxx, Isgarren, and any other threat that might draw on power levels beyond those of ""mortal"" individuals... But in the meantime, I will continue to refine my high score, as doing so increases the competitive instincts of my colleagues.",
"**Symbiosis of Progenitors and Spawn**  
After killing multiple titanspawn with his hunting party, lowland kodan Born from Thunder speculated that smaller spawned titans were acting as ancillary limbs for larger progenitor titans. Battle accounts from the former Pact commander and allies support these claims. We know these progenitor titans to be Greer, Decima, and, most recently, Ura.  
  
**Presence and Migration**  
The titanspawn are spread out across the isles and have made landfall across Lowland Shore, threatening the kodan inhabitants. While their exact origin point is still unknown, early sightings place them near Mount Balrior.  
  
**Inner Core Biology**  
Accounts from lowlander Rainless Meadow cited the progenitor titan Greer as having a glowing inner core, exposed upon damage to his body's exterior armor. A similar core was seen by the former Pact commander and allies during an altercation with Decima, suggesting a biological consistency. Both accounts note the core's sensitivity to outside forces.  
  
**Communication Practices**  
Accounts of the progenitor titans suggest an ability to communicate among themselves and with their opponents across great distances using storm-like calls and other interspecies communication. This information has been confirmed by Astral Ward scout Branwen, the former Pact commander, Caithe, and Malice.  
    
**Bloodstone: Restoration and Regeneration**   
Concerningly, accounts from Astral Ward member Paela claim that the progenitors and spawns alike seem highly drawn to bloodstone. Many have been spotted feeding upon bloodstone shards to promote regeneration, in addition to requiring additional rest. Similar findings were noted in field research conducted by the former Pact commander, Caithe, and Malice.",
"**The Chak**

---

After several weeks of searching our library, I finally found an obscure reference to the species that our people have discovered swarming around the ley root deep below Castora. The name was used during a testimonial by one of our scholars who was studying one of the native hylek tribes in the depths of the verdant peninsula. 

The scholar heard the hylek using the term ""chak"" in several different contexts, but always with the same meaning being ""a thing of ravenous appetites."" When they inquired further, the hylek merely pointed to one of the insectoid creatures, which some of them had strangely been cultivating as pets.

Having experienced the ""ravenous appetites"" of these creatures since the doyen introduced his most trusted to our subterranean territory in Leyspring Hollows, it is clear that the hylek are masters of laconicism. If anything, one could argue that these chak are the most insatiable creatures on the face of Tyria.

The way they hoard ley energy within themselves and excrete some sort of resinous goop is one of the most fascinating ecological case studies one could hope to dissertate upon. However, as that is not my area of study, the rest of these notes will detail my study of the chak in the context of their utility and the philosophical impact that they present to our kind.",
"**The Ecological Argument**

---

From an ecological perspective, then, the chak represent the greater of our dilemmas as it relates to our handling of these creatures.

Just as the Elder Dragons are known to feed on the world's magic during their cycle of awakening only to slumber when the greater part of Tyria's magic has been depleted, it is my supposition that the chak may perform a similar role in the periods when the dragons are asleep. In effect, whatever residual magic the Elder Dragons do not consume, the chak do, and they appear to have evolved to fulfill this very particular role as a part of the grand scheme of Tyria's magical balance.

To impact the development of the chak, or to impede their natural cycle, would pose an undue risk of upsetting that delicate balance. For what if the chak are, as part of their role in the magical ecosystem, partially or even wholly responsible for the Elder Dragons rising only every 10,000 years, give or take? The idea that these simple creatures might be that influential on the magical balance of this world may seem laughable, but I assure you that we are still not even sure what precise role the chak play in that system.

We know the chak feed on ley energy, but to what end? What else might they do to that magic? What is the connection between the ley energy they feed on and the resinous goop they excrete? These remain unanswered questions, though there are hypotheses that our teams are exploring.

For these reasons, and because this species clearly has a prior claim on this area, it is my recommendation, along with the rest of the scholars in my cohort, that our kind do **not** displace the chak from their native habitat. We should instead build **around** their nests and do our best not to interfere with these creatures. 

Our scholars will continue to study these chak, and our Navarium Core can coexist with the native species. On behalf of myself and the other scholars of the Indelible Word, we hope that the doyen, the council, and our other esteemed colleagues will heed these words.",
