"Doyen Sidony,  

I have no excuse for my candor in the diplomatic forums; I made a scene in front of you and your assembly, and in the olden memory of Gruna, your trusted friend, you deserved better than the grievances I heaped on you.  

I couldn't stomach the clan council's decision to heed your advice. Shrewd as it was, honorable it was not. Yes, some of the Forgotten ignored my ancestor's advice to avoid settling near the wellspring on the peninsula, but even then, their continued survival would have been preferrable. We are but prey to the dragons, and we should've put the fear of death in them when we had the chance. That is my belief, and I'll not shy away from it.  

That said, I've not the right or rank to offer support against the mursaat on behalf of my people. The council has been in tatters after the fire wyrm burrowed throughout the depths over the passing years. We are disorganized, digging through the rubble of the old world with nary a hint as to where the beast took to. Some clans sat pretty atop deep wells of ley magic, and they paid the steepest price despite Clan Way's warnings. Their townships have gone dark and silent. For now, many of the old routes, paths through the depths, are greatly changed or cut off for us.  

It's not what you wanted to hear, but it's the truth. We'll be lucky if the mursaat don't turn their spiteful gaze on us in time. We fell to your side, after all.  

My regrets,  
—Boward Waylit",
Doyen's Adytum,
Doyen's Adytum Waypoint,
"Doyen,  
  
Do not weep for me. I chose this. To give my soul for the preservation of your great works, that your secrets may forever endure...it is more than this astrologer deserves. There can be no higher privilege. No greater honor.  
  
Our star is grown ascendant by your wisdom. This, you know. I have read it for your eminence many times, here in this tower. When I meet the All Seer, I will carry with me the endless pride of having known and served you, my doyen.  
  
My friend. My master of ages. My dearest Sidony.  
  
Farewell,  
Your Raul",
"Doyen.  
  
There are few roles within seer society that are as deeply misunderstood as the role of doyen. The tome you hold in your hands will attempt to elucidate upon the misconceptions, vagaries, and unique responsibilities that are a doyen's burden to bear.  
  
---
  
### ""Immortal Leader""  
The first and primary misunderstanding of the doyen is that of the associated title, ""Immortal Leader."" While the reporting of the title is accurate, it does not, nor should it, convince any reader with more than a child's level of intellection that said title bestows the bearer with any greater lifespan than that with which they are naturally gifted. The title merely acknowledges that the role of doyen is taken on ""for life"" and passes only upon the bearer's death. As our kind are exceptionally long lived when compared against the lifespan of other lesser races, the usage of ""Immortal Leader"" becomes more than merely a symbolic gesture.

### Decision-Making
The second misconception that a doyen must endure is that they alone are the sole decision-maker for their people. Nothing could be further from the truth in that a doyen is only as wise and as influential as their council of elders. While it is true that a doyen's edict is seen as the guiding principle upon which seerkind relies, it is wholly mistaken to believe that the doyen's judgment is not heavily influenced by the members of their circle of advisors.

### The Mursaat
Another of the challenges that any doyen must face is the shared relationship with the mursaat, who the ignorant believe to be kin of seerkind. To think that our ennobled species could share common ancestry with those vermin from the Mists is a grave insult. ~~While there may be circumstances where our shared tenancy of this world requires us to temporarily ally with them~~ It falls to the doyen to do everything within their power to continue to maintain that separation, whatever the cost.",
