Justice,
Justice Duration Increase,
Justice is Blind,
"Justice is a concept that appears to be both universally understood and sought out by nearly all sentient species. The idea of being held accountable for one's actions, wherever those actions fall in the peaks and valleys of the moral landscape, is as old as Tyria itself, it seems.  
  
The notion of justice is a complicated one at its core, for what one society may deem morally good, another may perceive as the greatest of sins. Ultimately, without some objective guidance from an entity with perfect moral judgment, each culture must do its best to proceed according to its own tenets, established through the many millennia of their development.   
  
Complicating the notion of justice is the element of extended longevity, as is seen in our own kind: the seers. Our people can, and do, live for thousands of years. In that breadth of time, the worst of us might commit atrocities for hundreds of years, doing harm at a scale that would make the younger races of this world tremble to behold.  
  
What, then, is to be done to account for the seer lifespan as it pertains to the application of justice and punishment? Should the measures be applied proportionally?  
  
Perhaps this question will be perceived as meaningless to a number of our own kind, especially those who see the seers as being ""above"" the younger races, however we would be remiss if we believed ourselves to be wholly apart from these matters, as inevitably there will be a need for us to have some sort of methodology for approaching the topic if we are going to maintain relations with the other races of this world.",
