"_An Unofficial History of the Wizard's Court, Volume 1_   
  
by A. Hawthorn (signed 716 AE)  
  
Foreword, I suppose.  
  
Isgarren tasked me with writing out a complete history of the Wizard's Court by hand. I'm pretty sure this is torture for asking his age during the reading of my latest scroll translation—had that smug little grin on his face again. Forever kicking myself over not accepting Waiting Sorrow's invitation to join her at the Bastion of the Celestial.   
  
I've been sitting in the library for hours. Basic records, books of spells. Anything and everything you need to know about the potency of poisonous flora found in the Underworld. Things I ALREADY know, having served as the leading archivist for two decades. Nothing personal about Akeem, or Vass. Nothing about Isgarren's time before the court was founded.  
  
I suppose that raises the conundrum. I've been a leading archivist on the bastion for twenty-three years, and my knowledge is embarrassingly limited compared to his. Isgarren's ramblings about the prior cycle have always given me pause, but more recently, they have drawn my attention to the fact that he's kept that knowledge just out of reach from all but Mabon, maybe Sorrow. How isn't there a history of the court already?",
"_An Unofficial History of the Wizard's Court, Volume 2_    
  
by A. Hawthorn (signed 716 AE)    
  
The more dead ends I encounter while attempting to piece together a meaningful timeline of the foundation of the Wizard's Court to present, the more I realize: a history of the court would simply be a history of Isgarren himself.  
  
I am at least a little excited to venture down this path, as I've spent my entire time in the Astral Ward focusing on watching the continued escalation in Elona (for which Isgarren has shown little interest, which shocks me given his ""impassioned"" hatred for the demonic).  
  
In the absence of (attainable) records, I've decided to gather information on the curator by posing a question to the other chairs. Mabon, Waiting Sorrow, and Dagda—two of whom were most definitely from the same era as Isgarren himself, being from the ""elder races"" of Tyria.  
  
I spoke with Mabon first, given that he spends a great deal of time holed up in the private reading rooms of the library. He seemed bemused when I told him of Isgarren's little assignment. Shocked, even, but offered little beyond what we all already know: that Mabon was Isgarren's first ""recruit.""  
  
Mabon paused for a few moments, reflecting on that time. He noted the war between the seers and the mursaat. Isgarren ""spared him,"" supposedly, and for that Mabon will always be in his debt. He didn't say much else of note. He was quiet, and I expected him to be.",
"_An Unofficial History of the Wizard's Court, Volume 3_  
  
by A. Hawthorn (signed 716 AE)  
  
I expected Waiting Sorrow to be the more gentle of the three, but I was damn wrong. I encountered her attuning the conduits that keep Amnytas fixed in the clouds; usually mundane work, for someone of her skill, but on approach I noticed the furrow in her brow.  
  
Perhaps she was not in the mood to talk of the past. When I mentioned wanted to learn more of Vass and Akeem, her expression went blank. She watched me for a moment too long before simmering into a quiet rage; not at me, perhaps, but I'd certainly hit a nerve. ""Wouldn't it be nice, knowing what they were like?"" was all Sorrow muttered before taking her leave.   
   
Sorrow's assistants assured me that her foul mood was simply a product of long working hours. Repairs to the ring have been modest at best. I could tell from the dark patches under their eyes that they'd been working the same.",
"_An Unofficial History of the Wizard's Court, Volume 4_  
  
by A. Hawthorn (signed 716 AE)  
  
Catching Dagda in Amnytas is like sighting a rare bird in the wild. She's the only wizard that hasn't taken over a bastion, despite the Bastions of the Natural and the Obscure being without proper leadership. I've also had less than one conversation with her from start to completion, so... Yes, I'm a bit anxious. Especially after Sorrow's dismissal.  
  
I tracked her down in the Wizard's Tower; we were all stranded inside while Isgarren shifted Amnytas toward warmer skies for winter. She was sitting on the window ledge, watching the clouds as we traveled. I assume she'd heard of my impossible task, because she scooted left just enough to make room for another.  
  
""Sit."" And I sat. Despite not leading a bastion, Dagda's always had the commanding tone of a leader. I would follow her, I think.  
  
We sat in silence for a long while, watching the clouds. Dagda is very thoughtful, despite the rumors. Eventually, she spoke. She described to me ""that day,"" which I quickly parsed as being the moment Mabon and Waiting Sorrow saved her from the jotun. Her expression flickered from frustration to anger as she described the events (most of which, she readily acknowledged, Dagda learned from Mabon after her ascension; she only remembers faces, some names).  
  
She was the daughter of a female chieftain. One of the few female chieftains in jotun history. Another being born of magic, like her. The elders of the greater tribe feared Nuada's power—this was well after the age of jotun magic, after all—so they killed her. And most of Dagda's family. They probably would have killed Dagda, too, if she weren't so young (knowing what I do of the jotun, being collectors, likely wanted to keep those genetics intact; they likely viewed her and those powers as a tool).  
  
They stifled her magic as best they could. She doesn't remember those parts; doesn't want to. Her arms twitched as she spoke, possibly a reflexive response to things endured. Mabon found her while he and Sorrow were tracing migrations south (Jormag had ""birthed"" another champion, further displacing life in the north). That, she said, is most of it. She couldn't recount the rest, even if she wanted to.  
  
Before I took my leave, she touched my arm—a delicate gesture for such a warrior. She told me to be light when asking of the past. The wizards all chose to ascend and leave their past lives behind, but that doesn't mean they don't yearn for those threads. To reach out, and remember.",
"_Chapter 20_

""Is there a problem, beast?"" Olaf said, puffing out his chest. It was meant to intimidate the kodan, but to his dismay it only served to entangle Rising Hammer further. A moment of silence stretched between the two grizzled warriors, the kodan's claw remaining softly nestled in the norn's luxurious, rippling chest hair.

""I just realized,"" Rising Hammer said, breaking the silence with his quiet growl, ""your fur...is softer than mine.""

Olaf's heart skipped a beat. Through all their scuffles, their intense clashes of will, he'd thought of Rising Hammer as an obstacle to be overcome. But in reality, he was always the destination.

Somehow, this kodan had clawed his way into Olaf's heart. And now, being apart was unbearable.",
"_Chapter 2_

Diah sighed wistfully. So many had swung by Jeb's farm over the years; they'd tend to some corn, stomp some wurms, and then move on. Most didn't even stop to talk. It made Diah feel small. Unimportant. Just a pit stop on a longer journey.

But this asura lad, Rollek? He'd introduced himself immediately, smiling the whole time. And as she watched him go about his tasks, she could feel his joy in the simple farm work.

""Fascinating, just *fascinating*."" He muttered as he entertained one of her bovine, ""Their reactions imply a high level of intelligence not supported by any current hypothesis...""

No one had ever complimented the cows before. Not even Jeb.

Hm. Maybe Rollek would appreciate a slice of her Tasty Cake.",
"_Chapter 3_  
  
The tower stood tall and proud—as it had for generations—over the placid waters lapping against the flourishing docks of Gurrenhoof. Serena, a young buxom woman with rich auburn hair, gazed longingly up at the structure and imagined yet again her life among the clouds. Since she had been a small girl growing up in the small fishing hamlet she called home, her dreams and thoughts had bent toward the mysterious Wizard's Tower—so named by the town's leaders, past and present, for the watchful strong presence of Angurren, the legendary wizard of the tower.  
  
Serena sighed to herself, picturing the figure of the wizard as she imagined him in her mind. As rumor had it, Angurren had never been seen; some claimed he was human, others that he was of a species no one in Tyria had ever witnessed. She painted a mental image of him, with strong arms rippling with wiry, strong muscles. Serena imagined those arms enveloping her, protecting her as Angurren's tower protected their village. She breathed in the sea air, felt the warm breeze on her cheek, and envisioned that they were Angurren's caresses.  
  
""Someday,"" she muttered softly to herself. ""Someday I'll know the embrace of those arms.""  
  
Little did she know that her words, soft though they were, were carried lightly on the breeze, playing among the clouds surrounding the tower, until finally, gently circling into Angurren's sensitive ears. At hearing the trembling lilt in Serena's sweet voice, the wizened wizard's heart skipped a beat.  
  
Later that night, as Serena lay in her bed twirling one lock of hair and dreaming of Angurren, a shadow crossed the moon outside her window. She was startled upright, pulling the sheet over her body to cover her state of undress before realizing that the figure floating in the air outside her hovel was none other than the entity she had just been dreaming of a moment earlier: Angurren!  
  
He floated closer, tendrils of soft blue magic preceding him into the room through the window frame, snakelike and probing. They licked tenderly at her bare ankles protruding from beneath the sheet, sending shockwaves of delight up her legs, and she tingled.  
  
""I hope you don't mind my sudden...appearance,"" Angurren said. His voice echoed through the room and permeated every pore of her skin, and she shivered.  
  
""You're...the wizard,"" she heard herself stammer awkwardly, her nervousness stealing all rational thought from her mind.  
  
Angurren seemed unperturbed and floated closer to the window. His red eyes glowed warmly in the darkness and Serena felt her hands go slack, coming to rest by her side...the sheet sliding down the length of her torso. She smiled, finally gathering her courage as she began to rise from her bed.  
  
""I've always wanted to learn...magic.""  
",
"_Chapter 9_

Scarlet raised an eyebrow. ""You've seen how I play with toys I like, my petite pirate queen."" She cackled. ""I broke quite a few charr horns last week. And Duke Daren still hasn't recovered from the last time things got...hot...between us. Is this really what you want?""

Tai Min scoffed. Daren was pathetic. Of course she, Captain of the Etherknives, had no interest in being treated as Daren had: Stripped to her underwear. Thrown in a pot. Left to writhe for Scarlet's—

Her face flushed. ""No,"" she said quickly, cutting off her own escalating thoughts. ""No, you're right. We should probably...keep things professional.""

Scarlet grinned maniacally, and leaned in close to her pet pirate captain. ""Too late for that now, Tai. You'll never get me out of your head.""",
"_Entry 0342_  
  
The vizier is finally dead. That...saga has ended, and dawn has broken once again. There was a day where Isgarren thought to get involved in these global affairs once Legavo (the ""Scepter of Orr"") resurfaced, but the ward's numbers are still weak following Eparch's last push during the Century of Darkness. Hundreds of years, and we're still climbing out of that hole.  
   
For a long while, Isgarren suspected that Eparch was the one toying with the vizier's mind; for that reason, we remained close to the situation. Wayfinder Aziure became our eyes after the Searing forced us to move the Beacon of Ages to the north, where I write now. Aziure and the Order—led now by Sir Ardaric—have been our most frequent of allies, crossing paths pre- and post-Cataclysm. Our connections are largely severed today, despite their aid in keeping more...nefarious parties from the gates of the Wizard's Tower. Another chapter closed.     
  
Through our investigations we learned of Abaddon's manipulations; it wasn't Eparch who brought Nightfall upon Tyria, but one of them. [lbracket]REDACTED[rbracket] We aren't to get involved with any quandary of the ""gods."" [lbracket]REDACTED[rbracket] I am informed that these notations may be pulled at a later date.  
  
Following our own recovery efforts after the battle of [lbracket]REDACTED[rbracket], Mabon is headed to Janthir. We imagine that the mursaat are dwindling, and with a handful of titans still remaining on this side of the veil. He intends to establish a warding enchantment, though the reasoning for such has been limited to the court. The newly established Rift Hunters will also be using their reprieve from Astral Ward duties to venture into the Tyrian mainland in search of lingering Mistborn threats.      
  
End of record.     
  
*Recorded by Archivist Feven on the third day of the Season of Bloom*",
"_Foreword_  
   
*This journal is preserved as a part of Isgarren's collection of written relics. These documents display the thoughts of a significant figure in Tyria's history, whether they interacted with the court or not. Aziure was one of the most practiced of those bestowed the title of ""wayfinder"" and fought alongside the Order to protect the secrets of the Astral Ward during the Century of Prophecy.*   
  
Isgarren,  
  
I apologize for Verata's...extreme measures. He learned of our interactions following his expulsion from the Order, and I think jealousy is perhaps his most salient emotion. There was a time when I'd argue that he meant well, but I imagine his lust to learn the secrets of your tower got the better of him and his darling occultists. He never had the chance to walk through the Beacon of Ages before... Days long past, I believe. Ardaric is keeping a close eye on his camp in the meantime, in case the fellow gets any more...wise inklings. With Khilbron gone, Verata is quite bored. But that's a situation for us to handle. I imagine you've been quite busy since Komalie was sealed.       
   
With our mutual nightmare slain—or at the very least expelled from this plane of existence—Ascalon finally seems to be experiencing a moment of reprieve. Though, it is likely quite brief... The situation with the charr continues to burn, and our struggles are hardly diminished. I'm sure the ward's may also escalate depending on what comes of these events, but I wanted to thank you nonetheless.    
   
You have been an unlikely dawn after many long, dark nights. Even though the Chosen carried the torch to the brazier, having allies in this frightening, rapidly changing world will at least cushion the fall. Should you need our aid, the Order will fight with you again. Had you not pulled me from the flames that day... Well, I promise you that I'll never evoke the ""wizard's folly"" in anything but jest. Your secrets are safe. I just hope that I get to see the Beacon of Ages again. I miss it. I miss everything about the meadow. It's not the same.       
  
Aziure",
"_Needed Medical Supplies_  
  
Eddard, we should check with each of the medical stations and make sure that they have access to the following materials. The more I learn about healing Kryptis-touched patients, the more concerned I become over our lack of unconventional supplies. I've sent Lyhr's apprentices to the mainland to search for some of the needed herbs, but please check our reserves for the following materials:  
  
- Bandages  
- Sanitized gloves or hand coverings  
- Sage  
- Antidotes  
- Needles and thread  
- Dandelion blossoms  
- Emerald smoke*  
- Maguuma lilies  
- Night thistle  
- Garlic seeds  
- Restraints  
   
*Please speak with Zizel if you are unsure of what I'm asking for.",
"_OFFICE OF THE ARCANE COUNCIL_  
  
**FORM 0517b**: Research Disclosure  
  
**NAME**: Councillor Ludo  
  
**REASON**: Personal (  )    Official (**X**)  
  
**PURPOSE OF RESEARCH**:  
  
*The bottom of the sheet is covered in scribbles and doodles. Whoever filled this out was very, very bored.*",
"_Pact Commander Reconnaissance Log_  
  
1330 AE: Initial dispatch to Amnoon. Word quickly spread that the Pact commander was approaching Elona via airship. Logical, docking near the last semi-free commerce hub in the area. May this deployment not last long—the ward only supplied me with so many of these Joko statues to sell.
  
UPDATE: Contact established with target. Seemed very uninterested in my wares, but seems eager to continue their journey into the desert. Will keep an eye out and report back if anything goes against our expectations.  
  
UPDATE: Target has taken down the rogue god Balthazar. Worth continued observation as this event feels like an escalation to future conflicts. Requesting extended deployment to continue mission.
  
1331 AE: Target spotted in Domain of Kourna. Presumed quarry is Palawa Joko. Not sure how I should feel about this, but Isgarren's protection spell will protect me from anything that getting closer to the lich may throw at me.  
  
1332 AE: Target last reported to be on an island deep in the middle of the Sea of Sorrows. Maintaining contact with proxies on the ground in an area people are calling ""Dragonfall.""  
  
UPDATE: After the destruction of Kralkatorrik, I have received word that the target has been invited to a party in the far north, in the charr Blood Legion's homelands. Requesting permission to travel for closer observation.  
  
UPDATE: Contact established with target. Similar behavior: no signs of being interested in my wares, but I don't think they suspect anything. Proceeding as assigned.  
  
1333 AE: Updating after a longer break in established contact. Target's participation in the charr civil war and the subsequent mutual destruction of both Primordus and Jormag has lead the target to a bit of a respite.  
  
1335 AE: I've received word that the target was seen in...Cantha? That can't be right. Requesting permission for redeployment.  
  
UPDATE: Getting to Cantha has been a massive pain, even for me. It's like they've never seen an Awakened before. Managed to sneak in through an opened asura gate from Lion's Arch and am now waiting for contact with target inside Arborstone.
  
UPDATE: Contact established with target in Arborstone. Target appears to be assisting Canthans in defeating a new threat called the Void. Sending along word of this threat as one for the ward to monitor.  
  
UPDATE: EMERGENCY: The Void situation has become dire in Cantha. I am initiating an emergency evacuation back to Lion's Arch and will be returning to the Wizard's Tower until this all blows over.  
  
UPDATE: I've received word that the Void crisis in Cantha has finally receded. The world was on the brink, and the ward was ready to act, but the efforts of the target and their companions seem to have saved the day...for now. Will report back when I hear of any movement from the target. For now, I declare the mission to be complete.  
  
1336 AE: EMERGENCY: I am reopening this mission. The Wizard's Tower is being evacuated due to Kryptis threat. The tower itself has moved from its position near Garenhoff. It's only a matter of time before the target becomes involved now. I will be taking temporary shelter back in Lion's Arch for now to give those who can't fit in there the space they need. Bringing this log with me.  
  
UPDATE: I've received word that the target is now in Skywatch Archipelago, as expected. Monitoring the situation from Lion's Arch for now.  
  
UPDATE: The Wizard's Tower's defenses are stable and the ward is being allowed to return—and the target, newly designated as the wayfinder, is there, too. I'm going to head there tomorrow. I suspect it won't take long for the target to make contact with me once more. After all these years, it may finally be time to tell them what's been going on and drop the charade. It's the least I can do if they're going to save us. I'm going to prepare for how this conversation may go in my head. This will be my last update unless necessary. For now: mission complete.",
"_Page One_  
  
Rhea is dead. The twins care more about the killing than the purpose. This place was a beacon for five minutes, until it turned into a house of flagellantism.  
  
All of Gavril's citizens are gone now. A few managed to escape, but most of them died on the bloodstone. I don't even remember when the last one was killed... A year ago now? Two? We've been killing each other for so long, I can hardly separate killing for survival over killing for the ritual...  
  
What happened here? One day, we were following Saul's vision in pursuit of the golden city, and now I can't sleep without fear. Surely I lifted a brow when he said that he found such a city  tucked in the isles—even without proof, I stayed loyal. I followed. But when he asked us to stay behind on the order of the Unseen Ones...",
"_Page Three_  
  
I don't know how much longer I can hold out... All of the boats have been burned. The false gods and their ""golden city"" rest somewhere beyond the mountains, so I can't travel north.  
  
I've managed to stow myself in the tomb. Which is fitting. After the restoration, we told the villagers it was an inn. Pushed them inside for ""safety."" How many people died in here?  
  
Any day now, the twins or Manikaz or some other god-masked charlatan is going to pull me out of here by the hair and bleed me out over a rock. I hope whatever the Unseen Ones are running from tears them apart.",
"_Page Two_  
  
The Unseen Ones are a lie. I'll be struck down for writing such words, but it's the final truth I know. I don't know if they deceived Saul or if he was in on the prank, but this entire ordeal wasn't in the name of salvation—we will get nothing for the blood we've spilled. Not that the twins care about that, of course; they enjoy the sport.  
  
We are cattle and butcher. What is the bloodstone even for? What have we done? We've fed this thing for years now... Should've figured it out when supplies stopped coming from the mainland. Saul left us to die, whether he knew it or not.",
"_R'tchikk's List of Tasks_
  
- Return emptied potion vials to Zizel with haste (also compliment Zizel's attitude, skritt thinks this is a real boon in the middle of much chaos)
  
- Speak with new museum assistants and make sure that everyone is properly introduced to skritt
  
- Check with museum assistants on whether or not a new uniform is needed (skritt only wants to make a good impression, Glade says this is not important)  
  
- Speaking of Glade, skritt must keep an eye on her for signs of sadness (she is taking Mabon's passing with a heavy soul)
  
- On the subject of Mabon, skritt must help in the efforts to archive Mabon's belongings so that burden does not fall to the newer recruits  
  
- Remember to eat a hearty meal, R'tchikk does NOT want skritt's stomach angry with hunger next battle  
  
- Check in on Commander's well-being  
  
- Meet with Isgarren when he is unpossessed and ask for advice on handling museum tasks, skritt does NOT want to be caught staring at something shiny on the first day

**if* Isgarren is unpossessed",
"_Seite drei_  
  
Ich weiß nicht, wie lange ich noch durchhalten kann ... Alle Boote wurden verbrannt. Die falschen Götter und ihre ""goldene Stadt"" ruhen irgendwo jenseits der Berge; daher kann ich nicht nach Norden reisen.  
  
Es ist mir gelungen, mich im Grabmal zu verbergen. Wie passend. Nach dem Umbau machten wir den Dorfbewohnern weis, es handele sich um ein Gasthaus. Und wir haben sie ""zu ihrer Sicherheit"" hineinbeordert. Wie viele sind wohl hier drin gestorben?  
  
Es kann sich nur noch um Tage handeln, bis die Zwillinge, oder Manikaz, oder irgendein anderer Scharlatan mit Göttermaske mich hier an den Haaren herauszerrt und dann über einem großen Stein ausbluten lässt. Ich weiß nicht, wovor die Ungesehenen eigentlich weglaufen, aber ich hoffe, es reißt sie in Stücke.",
"_Seite zwei_  
  
Die Ungesehenen sind eine Lüge. Man wird mich dafür erschlagen, dass ich das so aufschreibe, aber es ist mir als die endgültige Wahrheit klar geworden. Ich weiß nicht, ob sie Saul täuschen konnten oder ob er mit ihnen gemeinsame Sache machte, aber all dieses Unrecht ist nicht im Namen des Heils und der Erlösung geschehen – wir werden für unser vergossenes Blut im Gegenzug gar nichts bekommen. Den Zwillingen ist das natürlich völlig egal; die sind einfach in ihrem Element.  
  
Wir sind wie Vieh und Schlächter. Wozu dient eigentlich der Blutstein? Was haben wir getan? Wir haben dieses Ding jetzt schon jahrelang genährt ... Wir hätten es uns denken können, als kein Nachschub mehr vom Festland eintraf. Saul hat uns dem sicheren Tod überlassen, ob ihm dies nun klar war oder nicht.",
_Skip Questions,
"___The Flamebearer___  
Since ancient days, before the previous dragonrise, the Flamebearer was a symbol of resilience against the harsh elements from the Far Shiverpeaks to the bitter northlands even further. Its significance grew in the times of Jormag's unrest. For most lowland folk, its deeper meaning is a vestige of our past connection to our people's namesake.  For the highlanders, the flame is deeply connected to their faith in Koda. His ""Blessing of Flame"" holds special significance to them. It can be seen from midwinter through early spring.  
  
___The Spearhead___  
Poised and true. Its guiding star always pointing us toward new adventures and challenges. One of the first constellations traced by our clan after our split from the highlanders. In more  recent history, it has become most emblematic of our people's mastery of the spear as both a tool and a weapon. It can be seen from midsummer through early autumn.  
  
___The Hammer___  
Another shared constellation. Representative of craft and the virtue of honest and challenging work. Both highland and lowland bearkin take great pride in their crafts, seeking mastery in talents recognized, in paths chosen. Regardless of time or talent, all must temper and shape their skills in the forge to reach such heights. It can be seen in the late spring through midsummer.  
  
___The Fisher___  
A symbol of our connection to the land and sea. A constant reminder of what we owe the natural world and the sustenance it provides. It can be seen in the night sky from late autumn through midwinter, correlating with migration of several species of fish to the headwaters.",
